    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to increasing the bonding authority of the New York city housing development corporation",
    "basePrintNo": "S7895",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7895-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §656, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7895\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the private housing finance law, in\nrelation to increasing the bonding authority of the New York city\nhousing development corporation\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill would authorize the New York City Housing\nDevelopment Corporation (\"HOC\") to increase the maximum aggregate\nprincipal amount of its outstanding bonds and notes from $11.25\nbillion to $12.5 billion.\n\nSection two would provide that this bill take effect immediately.\n\n REASONS FOR SUPPORT :\n\nBy the end of March 2016, HOC had approximately $10.4 billion of bonds\nsold or outstanding, leaving the corporation with slightly less than\n$850 million of unused bonding authority. HOC has also currently\nagreed to finance multi-year projects that will require an additional\n$133 million in bonds. In a little over a decade, since January\n1,2005, HOC has issued in excess of$15.6 billion in bonds to finance\nthe construction or preservation of tens of thousands of apartments\nthroughout the City. These include bonds to finance the preservation\nof thousands of Mitchell-Lama apartments as well as Liberty Bonds to\nfinance the construction or conversion from non-residential use of\nthousands of apartments in lower Manhattan. This record has made HOC\none of the largest issuers of multi-family affordable housing bonds in\nthe nation, and frequently the top annual issuer.\n\nUnder the City's \"Housing New York\" plan, as of April 1, 2016, HOC has\nfinanced the construction or preservation of more than 15,000\naffordable apartments in less than two years under the plan. HDC\nfinanced over 76,000 affordable apartments under the City's prior\nhousing plan, far surpassing its original goals. HOC financing\nrelating to the City's housing plan is provided under a variety of new\nprograms: (1) the Extremely Low and Low-Income Affordable program\n(\"ELLA\"), which provides apartments for low-income people earning 60%\nof the Area Median Income (\"AMP') or less, including deeply affordable\napartments for people earning no more than 30%, 40% and 50% of AMI or\napartments for the formerly homeless; (2) the Mixed- Middle Income\nprogram (\"M2\"), which provides apartments for people at a mix of\nincomes, including low income people, moderate-income people earning\n80-100% of AMI and middle-income people earning 130-165% of AMI; (3)\nthe \"Mix and Match\" program, which provides apartments for low-income\nand moderate- or middle-income people in the same project; (4) the\n\"50/30/20\" program, which provides apartments for low- and\nmoderate-income people in projects where half the project is rented at\nthe market rate; and (5) the Mitchell Lama restructuring program. In\naddition, HOC has launched a series of other initiatives that use the\ncorporation's bonding authority, including programs using taxable\nbonds and recycled tax-exempt bonds.\n\nThis legislation will strengthen HDC's ability to continue to provide\nfinancing for the creation and preservation of affordable housing in\nNew York City.\n\n FISCAL IMPACT :\n\nNone to state\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately\n",
